.TH DANBY-BANDS 1
.SH NAME
danby-bands \- why a solid has energies an electron cannot have
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danby/bands
.SH MODEL
One atom gives sharp levels;
.IR danby-hydrogen (1)
draws them.  A LINE of atoms, repeated for ever, gives something else
entirely: broad BANDS of allowed energy separated by GAPS that no
electron in the crystal can occupy at all.  That is the whole of why
there are conductors, insulators and semiconductors, and this is the
simplest model that produces it.
.PP
The lattice is a Dirac comb \(em a delta-function barrier of strength P
every a \(em which is the Kronig-Penney model.  Bloch's theorem says a
solution in a periodic potential must repeat up to a phase, and matching
across one barrier gives
.PP
.EX
cos(Ka) = cos(ka) + P sin(ka)/(ka),   k = sqrt(2E)
.EE
.PP
The left side is a cosine and CANNOT leave \-1 to 1.  The right side is
a function of the energy alone and happily does.  So an energy is
allowed only where the right side happens to land inside that range, and
is forbidden everywhere else.
.PP
Nothing here is approximated and nothing is numerical.  The gaps exist
because a cosine is bounded and the thing it is being set equal to is
not.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Danby/bands
is a
.IR danbylab (2)
script \(em the whole model is the text of
.BR /appl/danby/bands.spec .
.PP
The x axis is ENERGY, not time.  The model has no dynamics at all: it
sweeps E upward and evaluates a formula.  The one state it is obliged to
carry is put to use \(em it integrates the allowed indicator, so it
accumulates the total width of band passed so far.
.SS What to look for
The shaded regions are the bands.  Where the curve leaves \-1 to 1 the
shading stops, and that is a gap.
.PP
Gaps lose to bands as the energy rises, because the term that pushes the
function out of range carries a 1/ka and dies away: a fast electron
barely notices the lattice.  They do NOT shrink in absolute width \(em
measured at P = 3 and a = 1 they are 4.52 then 5.35 wide \(em but the
bands they separate widen much faster, 2.98 then 10.28 then 19.32.  The
first gap is one and a half times the band below it and the next only
half.  It is the RATIO that collapses, and the ratio is what decides
whether a material conducts.
.PP
Raising
.B strength
widens the gaps: a stronger lattice binds harder.  Turn it to zero and
every gap closes at once \(em with no barriers the function is just
cos(ka), which can never leave the range, so a free electron may have
any energy it likes.  That is the model's own control and the test makes
it.
.PP
Widening
.B lattice
moves every band down.  The edges sit at ka = n pi, so
E = n squared pi squared / 2a squared, and doubling the spacing quarters
every edge.
.SS The band edges are exact
At ka = n pi the sine vanishes, so the whole P term goes with it and the
function is exactly plus or minus one whatever P happens to be.  Those
energies are band edges for every lattice strength there is.  It is an
identity rather than a measurement, and
.IR danbylabtest (1)
holds it to nine places.
.SS The diagram
The lattice: a barrier every
.BR lattice ,
as tall as
.BR strength .
The frame is pinned, so shortening the spacing really does draw a
tighter comb rather than the same comb at a different zoom.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/danby/bands.spec
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danby (1),
.IR danby-hydrogen (1),
.IR danby-schrodinger (1),
.IR danbylab (2),
.IR danbylabtest (1)
.SH BUGS
A delta comb is not a solid.  Real lattices are three-dimensional, their
barriers have width, and the bands depend on direction; none of that is
here.  What survives the simplification is the part worth having \(em
that periodicity alone forbids some energies \(em and that part is
exact.
.PP
The drawn curve is clipped to plus or minus three.  Between bands the
function runs away, and without the clip the axis would rescale to it
and flatten every band on the screen.  The shading is computed from the
UNCLIPPED value, so the bands are in the right places even where the
curve is drawn flat along the clip.
.PP
At exactly ka = n pi the function is exactly plus or minus one, and
whether that counts as allowed depends on which side of one the
floating-point result lands.  The shading there is a coin toss on the
last bit.  It is a set of measure zero and the test does not ask.
.PP
.IR Danbylabtest (1)
checks the edge identity at three orders and two spacings, that zero
strength forbids nothing, that non-zero strength forbids something, the
1/a squared scaling, and the function against its closed form away from
the nodes.  That last check exists because the edge identity is BLIND:
at ka = n pi the sine is zero, so it cannot tell sin(ka)/(ka) from
sin(ka), and deleting the 1/ka \(em the reason gaps lose to bands, the
entire point of the model \(em passed every other check.
